\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Where CoCurve\xspace  sits.} Cross-module off-diag.: an explicit attention$\leftrightarrow $FFN or cross-layer curvature term. SAViT carries one but collapses each block to a scalar, so it acts at component-type rather than unit-pair granularity. SOSP's SOSP-I variant carries a full pairwise structure matrix, on homogeneous structures in convolutional networks (\autoref {app:related-ext}). FLAP adds a compensation bias rather than changing any retained weight, and SlimLLM regresses each sublayer's output matrix; both are post-pruning updates. Bonsai is the closest on estimation --- forward passes only, no weight update --- but fits an additive model with no pairwise term, over many rounds of re-estimation. Selection-only: no post-pruning weight update. Single-shot: no iterative re-estimation. Properties describe each method's native form --- ZipLM also has a one-shot mode, but its closed-form weight update keeps it off the selection-only column either way --- and in our experiments recovery is disabled for every method (\autoref {sec:exp-setup}). CoCurve\xspace  is the only row that holds all six.}}{17}{table.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:positioning}{{9}{17}{\textbf {Where \method sits.} Cross-module off-diag.: an explicit attention$\leftrightarrow $FFN or cross-layer curvature term. SAViT carries one but collapses each block to a scalar, so it acts at component-type rather than unit-pair granularity. SOSP's SOSP-I variant carries a full pairwise structure matrix, on homogeneous structures in convolutional networks (\cref {app:related-ext}). FLAP adds a compensation bias rather than changing any retained weight, and SlimLLM regresses each sublayer's output matrix; both are post-pruning updates. Bonsai is the closest on estimation --- forward passes only, no weight update --- but fits an additive model with no pairwise term, over many rounds of re-estimation. Selection-only: no post-pruning weight update. Single-shot: no iterative re-estimation. Properties describe each method's native form --- ZipLM also has a one-shot mode, but its closed-form weight update keeps it off the selection-only column either way --- and in our experiments recovery is disabled for every method (\cref {sec:exp-setup}). \method is the only row that holds all six}{table.caption.14}{}}
